.TH CONFINE 1 2026-10-28 "agentutils 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
confine \- run a command that may write only to a directory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B confine
.RB [ \-w
.IR path ]...
.RB [ \-d
.IR dir ]
.RI [ command
.RI [ argument ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B confine
runs
.I command
in
.I dir
(the working directory if not given),
or your shell
.RB ( $SHELL ,
or
.B /bin/sh
if it is not set) if no command is given, confined: the command may write
only to
.IR dir ,
and may not read secrets.
The processes it starts are confined the same way, and none can undo it.
.PP
.B confine
does not stay: it becomes the command, which so exits as it would, with its
own status, or by the signal that ended it.
.PP
Everything from
.I command
on belongs to the command, its own options included, so
.B confine claude \-\-resume
passes
.B \-\-resume
to
.BR claude .
.PP
.B confine
knows nothing about forks: it confines a command to any directory.
Together with
.BR fsfork (1),
the command changes only a fork of the directory, which
.BR fsdiff (1)
then shows the changes in.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-d " dir" "\fR, \fP\-\-dir " dir
Run the command in
.I dir
and let it write there, rather than in the working directory.
.TP
.BI \-w " path" "\fR, \fP\-\-write " path
Let the command write to
.I path
too, and to everything under it.
Can be repeated.
Commands that keep state in the home directory need it: for example,
.B confine \-w ~/.claude claude
lets Claude Code save its sessions and settings.
.SH WHAT THE COMMAND MAY DO
Everything but:
.TP
Writing
Only
.IR dir ,
the temporary directories
.RB ( /tmp
and
.BR $TMPDIR ),
devices, the caches that builds and package managers write to
.RB ( ~/.cargo ,
.BR ~/.rustup ,
.BR ~/.cache ,
.B ~/.npm
and
.BR ~/Library/Caches ),
and the paths given with
.B \-w
may be written to.
.TP
Reading
The contents of
.BR ~/.ssh ,
.BR ~/.aws ,
.B ~/.gnupg
and
.B ~/.config/gh
cannot be read.
On Linux, their files can still be listed, and files made in the
directories above them after the command starts cannot be read.
.PP
The network and the GPU are not confined.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B confine
sets
.B CLAUDE_CODE_SANDBOXED
to
.B 1
for the command, so that Claude Code trusts
.I dir
without asking: it would otherwise ask in every new directory, and
accepting would write to
.IR ~/.claude.json ,
which it may not.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command's, as it becomes the command.
If it cannot run the command,
.B confine
exits with 127 if the command was not found and 126 otherwise, as
.BR env (1)
does, and with 1 if
.I dir
or a path given with
.B \-w
does not exist.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run Claude Code in a fork of the working directory:
.PP
.RS
.B confine \-w ~/.claude \-d $(fsfork) claude
.RE
.PP
Run a shell that may write only to the working directory:
.PP
.RS
.B confine
.RE
.SH CAVEATS
On macOS, the command runs under Seatbelt, through
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
which cannot run inside another sandbox, so a confined command cannot
confine another.
On Linux, it runs under Landlock, which needs Linux 5.19 or later; without
it,
.B confine
fails rather than run the command unconfined.
The command cannot run set-user-ID programs such as
.BR sudo (8)
on Linux.
.PP
The network is not confined: a command can send anything it can read to
anywhere, and can push to remotes whose credentials it can reach, such as
through the macOS keychain or an SSH agent.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fsdiff (1),
.BR fsfork (1),
.BR sandbox\-exec (1),
.BR landlock (7)
